## Proof

**Answer: Yes, such a $G$-equivariant bijection must exist.**

Since $G$ acts freely on $X$, every orbit $Gx$ is in bijection with $G$ via $g \mapsto gx$ (injectivity is exactly freeness). Hence each orbit has cardinality $|G|$, and the orbits partition $X$. Let $\kappa = |G|$ (infinite by hypothesis), $\alpha = |X/G|$, $\beta = |Y/G|$. By the partition,

$$|X| = \kappa \cdot \alpha, \qquad |Y| = \kappa \cdot \beta.$$

For cardinal arithmetic with $\kappa$ infinite and $\alpha,\beta \geq 1$,

$$\kappa \cdot \alpha = \max(\kappa, \alpha), \qquad \kappa \cdot \beta = \max(\kappa, \beta).$$

The hypothesis gives $|X| = |Y| > \kappa$. From $|X| = \max(\kappa,\alpha) > \kappa$ we must have $\alpha > \kappa$, so $|X| = \alpha$. Likewise $|Y| = \max(\kappa,\beta) > \kappa$ forces $\beta > \kappa$, so $|Y| = \beta$. Therefore

$$\alpha = |X| = |Y| = \beta,$$

i.e. the orbit sets $X/G$ and $Y/G$ have the same cardinality.

Now choose any bijection $\varphi: X/G \to Y/G$ between the orbit sets. For each orbit $\mathcal{O} \in X/G$, both $\mathcal{O}$ and $\varphi(\mathcal{O})$ are free $G$-torsors (free transitive $G$-sets). Pick a representative $x_{\mathcal{O}} \in \mathcal{O}$ and $y_{\mathcal{O}} \in \varphi(\mathcal{O})$, and define

$$f|_{\mathcal{O}}(g\, x_{\mathcal{O}}) := g\, y_{\mathcal{O}} \quad (g \in G).$$

This is well-defined (freeness makes $g \mapsto gx_{\mathcal{O}}$ a bijection $\mathcal{O}\to G$), bijective on each orbit, and $G$-equivariant by construction. Patching over all orbits yields a bijection $f: X \to Y$ with $f(gx) = g f(x)$ for all $g \in G$, $x \in X$. $\blacksquare$

